    "case_id": "500",
    "case_summary": "An 83-year-old woman presented with worsening left lower extremity pain, swelling, and erythema for the past few months. Previously independent in activities of daily living 2-3 months ago, she had become fully dependent on her daughter due to severe lower extremity pain. No history of trauma, recent travel, fever, chills, nausea, or vomiting. Medical history included stage I breast cancer in remission for 8 years after lumpectomy, radiation, and anastrozole therapy. Current medications included hydrochlorothiazide, gabapentin, montelukast, and albuterol. Former tobacco smoker. Had received radiation followed by immunotherapy, previously treated with nivolumab and sotorasib, and began new medication 3 months before presentation. Lower extremity erythema began a couple of weeks after first cycle, with last cycle given 3 weeks before current presentation.\n\nPatient was afebrile and normotensive with normal heart rate. Bilateral lower extremities were hyperpigmented with significant pitting edema. Left lower extremity had erythematous, ill-defined, and extremely tender plaques from toes to knee. Right lower extremity had smaller indurated plaques on the posterior ankle. Crepitus, fluctuance, purulence, and ulcers were absent.\n\n- Laboratory tests:\n  - Hemoglobin: 89 g/L (reference range, 114–144 g/L)\n  - Mean corpuscular volume: 100.7 fL (reference range, 79.4–94.8 fL)\n  - C-reactive protein: 72.8 mg/L (reference range, <10.0 mg/L)\n  - Folate: 35.5 nmol/L (reference range, >13.4 nmol/L)\n  - Vitamin B12: 824.9 pmol/L (reference range, 132.8–764.3 pmol/L)\n\n- Imaging studies:\n  - Bilateral lower extremity x-rays\n  - Contrasted computed tomography angiogram run-off\n  - Venous reflux study\n  - Multiple venous Doppler ultrasonography studies\n  Results showed moderate peripheral arterial disease and mild soft tissue swelling of the left distal calf and foot\n\n- Images:\n  Figure 1: Acute lipodermatosclerosis-like eruption: physical examination findings\n  Description: Shows progression of condition in left leg from initial findings to current presentation, and current presentation of right leg",
